= The question, and the answer in brief <ch-question>

This guide answers one question:

#block(inset: (left: 14pt, y: 4pt), stroke: (left: 2pt + hair))[
  #text(size: 12pt, style: "italic")[Once artificial intelligence automates mental and physical labor entirely, what happens to our economy?]
]

It is an easy question to answer badly, and most answers go wrong in one of three ways.

The first is to reason from the past without knowing why the past turned out as it did. Machines have replaced human work for two hundred years and workers became richer, so they will again. That is a pattern, and patterns hold until the conditions behind them change. @ch-history finds the conditions.

The second is to confuse making things with sharing them out. People say that if nobody has a wage nobody can buy anything, so the economy will collapse, or automation will stop itself. Neither follows. An economy can produce in abundance and still leave most people with no claim on the product. @ch-distribution shows why these are separate matters.

The third is to confuse what machines can do with what they are being used to do. One camp looks at the capabilities of AI and announces that everything changes next year. The other looks at the unemployment rate and announces that nothing is happening. @ch-now shows that both are describing the same world.

== How the guide is built

The guide is meant to be checked as well as read. It is built as a chain, and the links are numbered.

- An *axiom* is a basic fact about how economies work, stated with the evidence for it. There are nine.
- A *definition* fixes the meaning of a term.
- A *proposition* is a conclusion. Each one names the earlier statements it rests on, so that a reader who doubts it can see exactly which link to test.
- A *forecast* is a judgment about the future, with a probability attached. Forecasts appear only in Part IV and are kept apart from everything else.

Statements are referred to by letter and number: A3 is the third axiom, P12 the twelfth proposition. In the electronic version each reference is a link. Every chapter ends with a box that says where the chain stands, and Appendix B lists the whole chain on two pages.

The guide has four parts. Part I sets out how a market economy distributes what it produces, and why earlier automation raised wages. Part II assumes that machines can do all work and derives what happens to wages, to production and to the distribution of income. Part III asks who has the power to change that distribution. Part IV leaves the assumption behind, looks at the evidence as of November 2026, builds six scenarios for the next ten years and ends with my own best guess.

== The answer in brief

For readers who want the conclusion before the argument, here it is. Every sentence below is defended in the chapter named.

+ In a market economy, people receive a share of what is produced by owning something that production needs. Most people own little except their capacity to work, so wages are how most people get their share (@ch-economy).
+ Machines made workers richer for two centuries because every machine was narrow. There was always something left that only people could do, and whatever is scarce is well paid (@ch-history).
+ A machine that can do everything a person can do removes that protection. The wage for any work then cannot exceed what it costs to have the machine do it, and that cost keeps falling. This happened once before, to horses (@ch-wages).
+ The effect is not gradual. While any necessary task still needs people, the people who do it can prosper. When none does, the gain reverses. "Nearly all" and "all" are different worlds (@ch-wages).
+ Production does not suffer. An economy with machine labor can produce far more than ours, and grow faster (@ch-growth).
+ Nor does it run out of customers. The income that used to be paid as wages is paid to owners, and production turns to serve them and to build more machines. The economy keeps running. It stops providing for most people (@ch-distribution).
+ Some paid work remains wherever a person is wanted as a person. It reflects how income is distributed and cannot repair it (@ch-residual).
+ Majorities have had a share of output because they were needed as workers, soldiers and taxpayers. Automation removes that leverage over time, so any lasting claim on the output of machines has to be secured during the transition (@ch-power).
+ Providing for everyone would cost a small fraction of what such an economy produces. Claims held as property last; claims that must be voted again each year do not (@ch-policy).

That is the answer to the question as asked. Its short form is: *the economy does not break; the wage does, and the wage was doing more than anyone noticed.*

The forecast for the coming decade is more uncertain and is argued in Part IV. In short: machines will probably be able to do nearly all screen-based work around the end of this decade, and will take a further ten years or so to be put fully to that use. Robots capable of general physical work will follow roughly a decade behind. The change will show up first as a collapse in hiring, then as a recession from which office jobs do not return. By 2036 the United States will most likely be part of the way through: machines doing perhaps a quarter of all work, employment and labor's share of income each down by several points, policy a patchwork of dividends, public stakes and protected jobs, and the question of who owns the machine economy unsettled. I give that central scenario two chances in five, faster ones one in four, slower ones a little under one in four, and a well-managed one about one in eight.

== What the guide leaves out

It is about the economy of the United States, with other countries mentioned in passing. It treats the dangers of advanced AI that are not economic, such as loss of human control over the systems or their use as weapons, only where they bear on the argument. They may well matter more than anything discussed here. And it is written at one date. The facts in Part IV are those of 12 November 2026, and some will be out of date within months.
